  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 107 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nRight now a large closed upper level low is swirling over\nnortheastern Kansas while a negatively titled shortwave trough\nswirls to its southeast into Louisiana and the northern Gulf.\nAhead of the trough a strongly baroclinic zone is becoming even\nmore tightly aligned and frontogenetic forcing and jet level\nascent are leading to rain, mixed with sleet at times across\nroughly the western 2/3rds of the area. Wet-bulb temperatures in\nPerry and Bollinger County, MO is close to freezing already and\ncooler/drier air from the northwest that will get pulled in as\nwinds shift to the northwest should further help temperatures\ndrop. Precipitation intensity should continue to increase over\nthe next 2-3 hours as lift starts to maximize and a band about\n40-50 miles wide of heavier precip should start to become\nevident by 3 or 4 pm.\n\nHeight falls/dynamical cooling and wet-bulb cooling with dry \nair advection should drop temperatures sufficiently in most of \nthe winter storm warning area to switch to snow by late \nafternoon/early this evening. Lift and moderate to heavy precip \nthen continues as the aformentioned upper low slides just \nsouthwest of the area through the overnight hours. The \ndeterministic forecast has 8-10 inch accumulations in parts of \nPerry/Bollinger/Wayne Counties with 4-6 inches into the rest of \nthe winter storm warning area. Ensemble/probabilistic guidance \nstill has about a 60% chance of 6+ inches from Greenville, MO up\nto Perryville, MO. The expected snowfall gradient is still \nexpected to remain sharp. For example the deterministic forecast\nhas 6-8 inches in far western Cape Girardeau County with 1-2 \ninches on the eastern edge of the county. Rain and snow wraps \naround the departing system tomorrow during the day with \ntemperatures still looking likely to move above freezing area- \nwide as heights rise amid somewhat unusual low-level warm \nadvection behind the system. \n\nAnother storm system is coming into better model agreement on\nSunday with a surface low at 1000-1002mb passing nearby and\ngenerating rain and possibly a few isolated\nthundershowers(elevated). Deep frontal inversions into the Gulf\nand even Caribbean should significantly limit available \nmoisture return/heat which look to mitigate any surface-based \ninstability ahead of the system. Seasonable conditions then look\nto follow behind that system with highs in the low 50s and lows\nnear or slightly below freezing.",
